---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const H = {
  short: "Files never change; records change by revision",
  how: "Where each version is fixed",
  rules: "Signatures, runs, exports and artifacts",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      This page answers: when data changes, what happens to the old version, and how does Iki prove
      which version something used? A file never changes: its content is fixed by its SHA-256, or,
      for a file registered in place, by the object version Iki pinned (<PageLink
        to="Where data is stored"
      />). A new version of a file is a new file. Putting it in a record's field is an edit, so it
      makes a new revision of the record, and the old revision still names the old file. Signatures,
      runs and exports each bind exact revisions and file hashes, so later changes can't alter what
      they say they read.
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>How it's fixed</th><th>Where it's described</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>A file's bytes</td>
            <td>Content-addressed blob, or a pinned object version; append-only rows (<Adr n="0036" />)</td>
            <td><PageLink to="Where data is stored" /></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Which file a record held</td>
            <td>The field's value in each revision's history row; <code>FileUse</code> keeps the revision that first used it</td>
            <td><PageLink to="Revisions and history" />, <PageLink to="How data links to records" /></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>A field that must never change</td>
            <td><code>immutable: true</code>, as a read set's FASTQ files are (<Path p="plugins/sequencing/types/read_set.yaml" />)</td>
            <td><PageLink to="Saving and validation" /></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>What a signer approved</td>
            <td>The SHA-256 of the record's content at that revision, which includes each file's SHA-256</td>
            <td><PageLink to="Electronic signatures" /></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>What a run read</td>
            <td>Its inputs' revisions and its files' IDs and hashes, pinned at launch</td>
            <td><PageLink to="Pinning and provenance" /></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      A file's page shows, for each record that held it, the span of revisions that did: from the
      revision that set it to the one before the revision that took it out, or to now (<Adr
        n="0177"
      />). A wrong file is never overwritten; the record is edited to hold the right one, and both
      stay in its history.
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="A signature binds file hashes" open>
        <p>
          The signed content (<code>iki/signed-content/v1</code>) is the record's values as its history
          tables keep them, its references, and the SHA-256 of each file it holds, including files in
          list rows (<Adr n="0126" />, <Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/signatures/services.py"
            sym="def content_of("
          />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/signatures/proof.py" sym="def content(" />). An
          experiment's signatures also bind the hashes of its direct files (<Adr n="0189" />). Verifying
          a bundle offline checks each signed file's hash against the bundle's history (<Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/signatures/offline.py"
            sym="wrong_files"
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="A run reads the versions it was launched with">
        <p>
          A job's files are listed by ID, size and SHA-256 when it's leased, from the revisions its
          inputs were launched on, and the worker checks each download before the command starts
          (<Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/input_files.py" />). Any run can be reproduced exactly
          from what it recorded (<Adr n="0057" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Exports name their versions">
        <p>
          A record file is written from one revision, so the same revision is always the same file,
          and its SHA-256 is audited. A search CSV reads each page in one snapshot, and an audit-trail
          CSV holds the revisions as of its export event (<Adr n={[171, 207]} />, <PageLink
            to="Exports"
          />). A bundle carries each blob once by SHA-256, and a registered file as its location,
          version and hash (<Adr n="0096" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Artifacts keep their hash after their bytes">
        <p>
          A run's artifacts are deleted when their retention ends, but the record of each, with its
          path, size and SHA-256, stays (<Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/artifacts.py" />, <Adr
            n="0068"
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
